1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,153
In this video, we'll learn
coordinates on our round Earth,

2
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:08,006
and see how meteorology uses
them to measure distances and

3
00:00:08,006 --> 00:00:09,800
areas on its weather grids.

4
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:16,125
How do we say where a place is on a round
Earth, and how far apart two places are?

5
00:00:16,475 --> 00:00:19,697
This is the third of seven
parts on coordinates,

6
00:00:19,697 --> 00:00:23,925
and in this part, we leave the flat
map and go round the globe.

7
00:00:24,275 --> 00:00:29,675
Let's see how latitude and longitude are
built, and how they turn into kilometres.

8
00:00:30,025 --> 00:00:34,214
Then let's see what they tell us
about the grid boxes of weather data,

9
00:00:34,214 --> 00:00:37,325
and about how much of the Earth
lies in the tropics.

10
00:00:38,125 --> 00:00:40,500
First, a quick look back.

11
00:00:40,850 --> 00:00:45,673
In Part 1, we met polar coordinates
on a flat map: a distance r,

12
00:00:45,673 --> 00:00:51,175
and an angle theta, measured anticlockwise
from east, as seen from above.

13
00:00:51,525 --> 00:00:56,900
We found that x equals r cosine
theta, and y equals r sine theta.

14
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,153
We met radians, and the arc length rule:

15
00:01:00,153 --> 00:01:04,725
the length of an arc is the radius
times the angle, in radians.

16
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:10,925
In Part 2, we watched arrows turn, and we
found the slopes of sine and cosine.

17
00:01:11,275 --> 00:01:13,900
When an angle grows by a tiny step,

18
00:01:13,900 --> 00:01:17,725
its sine grows by about
its cosine times that step.

19
00:01:18,075 --> 00:01:21,985
We saw that air going round a ring
is always accelerating,

20
00:01:21,985 --> 00:01:24,075
towards the centre of the ring.

21
00:01:24,425 --> 00:01:28,410
Then we added a height z, to get
cylindrical coordinates,

22
00:01:28,410 --> 00:01:31,975
and looked inside Hurricane
Ian and Cyclone Amphan.

23
00:01:32,325 --> 00:01:37,075
Now let's take these ideas to the biggest
round shape of all: the Earth.

24
00:01:37,875 --> 00:01:39,875
Let's start with the shape.

25
00:01:40,225 --> 00:01:44,825
The Earth is very nearly a ball, a
sphere, so let's treat it as one.

26
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:48,550
On the flat map, we
measured from an origin.

27
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:52,750
On the Earth, the natural
origin is the Earth's centre.

28
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:56,133
Then any point, on the
ground, up in the air,

29
00:01:56,133 --> 00:01:59,975
or deep underground, can be
pinned down by three numbers.

30
00:02:00,325 --> 00:02:06,400
The first is its distance from the centre,
which we call r, just as on the flat map.

31
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:09,734
The other two are
angles, which say in which

32
00:02:09,734 --> 00:02:13,125
direction the point lies, as seen
from the centre.

33
00:02:13,475 --> 00:02:18,375
A distance and two angles like this
are called spherical coordinates.

34
00:02:18,725 --> 00:02:23,028
On the Earth, the two angles
are the ones you know from maps:

35
00:02:23,028 --> 00:02:24,650
latitude and longitude.

36
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,175
But the centre is hidden
deep inside the Earth.

37
00:02:28,525 --> 00:02:31,500
So to see it, let's cut the Earth open.

38
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:35,256
Imagine slicing the Earth
in half with a flat

39
00:02:35,256 --> 00:02:37,949
knife that passes through the North Pole,

40
00:02:37,949 --> 00:02:42,875
the South Pole, and the city of Brest, a
port at the western tip of France.

41
00:02:43,225 --> 00:02:45,713
Now we can see the flat face of the cut,

42
00:02:45,713 --> 00:02:48,450
with the Earth's centre
in the middle of it.

43
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,975
Let's draw an arrow
from the centre to Brest.

44
00:02:52,325 --> 00:02:56,000
Its length is Brest's
distance from the centre, r.

45
00:02:56,350 --> 00:03:00,625
For any place on the ground, that
length is the Earth's radius.

46
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:04,219
It's about six thousand
four hundred kilometres,

47
00:03:04,219 --> 00:03:08,275
or more exactly, six thousand
three hundred and seventy one.

48
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:14,100
It's used in so many formulas
that it gets its own letter.

49
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:16,125
We call it a.

50
00:03:16,475 --> 00:03:19,975
So for places on the ground, r equals a.

51
00:03:20,325 --> 00:03:23,281
A weather balloon ten
kilometres up is only

52
00:03:23,281 --> 00:03:25,962
ten kilometres farther from the centre,

53
00:03:25,962 --> 00:03:28,300
about a sixth of one percent more.

54
00:03:28,650 --> 00:03:30,474
That's why, for the weather,

55
00:03:30,474 --> 00:03:34,775
we can usually use the Earth's
radius for every height in the air.

56
00:03:35,575 --> 00:03:38,125
Now let's measure the first angle.

57
00:03:38,475 --> 00:03:42,950
On the cut face, draw a line
from the centre out to the equator.

58
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,179
Latitude is the angle
between that line and

59
00:03:46,179 --> 00:03:49,325
the arrow to the place,
measured at the centre.

60
00:03:49,675 --> 00:03:52,400
We write it with the Greek letter phi.

61
00:03:52,750 --> 00:03:56,441
For Brest, the arrow is turned
up from the equator line

62
00:03:56,441 --> 00:03:59,175
by about forty eight point four degrees.

63
00:04:00,675 --> 00:04:05,150
That's why we say Brest is at forty
eight point four degrees north.

64
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:08,050
At the equator, phi is zero.

65
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:13,100
Halfway from the equator to the North
Pole, it's forty five degrees.

66
00:04:13,450 --> 00:04:17,325
At the North Pole
itself, it's ninety degrees north.

67
00:04:17,675 --> 00:04:23,150
At the South Pole, it's ninety degrees
south, which we write as minus ninety.

68
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:27,367
So latitude says how far
north or south a place is,

69
00:04:27,367 --> 00:04:30,475
from minus ninety to plus ninety degrees.

70
00:04:31,275 --> 00:04:34,350
Which other places share Brest's latitude?

71
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:37,800
Let's put the Earth back
together and turn it.

72
00:04:38,150 --> 00:04:41,055
Brest's arrow sweeps
round the Earth's axis,

73
00:04:41,055 --> 00:04:45,345
always at forty eight point four
degrees above the equator plane,

74
00:04:45,345 --> 00:04:48,250
and its tip traces a circle
round the globe.

75
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:53,100
Every place on that circle has
the same latitude as Brest.

76
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:58,475
This circle is called a parallel,
because it runs parallel to the equator.

77
00:04:58,825 --> 00:05:05,150
Brest's parallel runs east through central
Europe, Ukraine, Kazakhstan and Mongolia.

78
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:09,592
Then it runs on round through
the northern United States and

79
00:05:09,592 --> 00:05:13,275
eastern Canada, and back
across the Atlantic to Brest.

80
00:05:13,625 --> 00:05:17,309
Now let's slice the Earth
the other way: straight across,

81
00:05:17,309 --> 00:05:20,800
through Brest's parallel,
at right angles to the axis.

82
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:25,750
The cut face is a flat
disc, and its rim is Brest's parallel.

83
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:29,625
The centre of this disc
is not the Earth's centre.

84
00:05:29,975 --> 00:05:33,875
It sits on the Earth's
axis, above the equator plane.

85
00:05:34,225 --> 00:05:37,775
So a parallel is a circle
round the Earth's axis.

86
00:05:38,125 --> 00:05:42,528
The equator is the biggest parallel,
and the parallels get smaller and

87
00:05:42,528 --> 00:05:46,050
smaller towards the poles, where
they shrink to a point.

88
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:49,850
We'll measure the size
of Brest's parallel very soon.

89
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:54,075
There's a second way
to measure the same direction.

90
00:05:54,425 --> 00:05:57,525
You'll meet it in many
maths and physics books.

91
00:05:57,875 --> 00:06:01,773
Instead of starting from the equator,
start from the North Pole,

92
00:06:01,773 --> 00:06:05,550
and measure the angle down
from the Earth's axis to the arrow.

93
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:08,800
This angle is called the colatitude.

94
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:09,988
Latitude and

95
00:06:09,988 --> 00:06:15,575
colatitude together fill the right angle
between the equator plane and the axis.

96
00:06:15,925 --> 00:06:20,225
So the colatitude is ninety
degrees minus the latitude.

97
00:06:20,575 --> 00:06:24,256
For Brest, it's ninety
minus forty eight point four,

98
00:06:24,256 --> 00:06:26,875
which is forty one point six degrees.

99
00:06:28,375 --> 00:06:31,450
Be careful not to mix up two words here.

100
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:35,550
The colatitude is an angle,
measured at the centre.

101
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:40,875
A parallel is a circle, made of all
the places with the same latitude.

102
00:06:41,675 --> 00:06:44,825
Why does it matter
which angle a book uses?

103
00:06:45,175 --> 00:06:48,200
Because sine and cosine swap places.

104
00:06:48,550 --> 00:06:51,778
The next minute and a half
shows exactly why,

105
00:06:51,778 --> 00:06:54,575
for anyone who likes to see every step.

106
00:06:54,925 --> 00:06:57,891
You don't need it
for the intuition of weather,

107
00:06:57,891 --> 00:07:02,750
climate science or mapping, so feel free
to skip ahead to the time on screen.

108
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:05,875
Let's see why, on one triangle.

109
00:07:06,225 --> 00:07:09,900
Take the arrow to Brest, but
shrink it to length one.

110
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:16,100
From its tip, drop a line straight down to
the equator plane, parallel to the axis.

111
00:07:16,450 --> 00:07:20,725
Together with the equator
line, this makes a right triangle.

112
00:07:21,075 --> 00:07:26,353
From Part 1, the side next to an angle
is the long side times the cosine,

113
00:07:26,353 --> 00:07:30,475
and the side opposite it
is the long side times the sine.

114
00:07:30,825 --> 00:07:34,525
At the centre, the angle
is the latitude, phi.

115
00:07:34,875 --> 00:07:39,875
The side next to it lies along
the equator plane, so it's cosine phi.

116
00:07:40,225 --> 00:07:45,025
The side opposite it runs parallel
to the axis, so it's sine phi.

117
00:07:45,375 --> 00:07:49,750
Now look from the triangle's other
sharp corner, at Brest's end.

118
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:55,100
The three angles of a triangle add
up to a hundred and eighty degrees.

119
00:07:55,450 --> 00:08:00,575
The right angle takes ninety, so the two
sharp angles share the other ninety.

120
00:08:00,925 --> 00:08:05,093
So the angle at Brest's end
is ninety degrees minus phi,

121
00:08:05,093 --> 00:08:07,475
which is exactly the colatitude.

122
00:08:07,825 --> 00:08:12,482
From that corner, the side next to it
is the one parallel to the axis,

123
00:08:12,482 --> 00:08:16,275
and the side opposite it
is the one in the equator plane.

124
00:08:16,625 --> 00:08:21,450
Same triangle, same sides, but
next and opposite have swapped.

125
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:25,177
So the sine of the colatitude
equals cosine phi,

126
00:08:25,177 --> 00:08:28,625
and the cosine of the colatitude
equals sine phi.

127
00:08:28,975 --> 00:08:34,912
For Brest, the sine of forty one point six
degrees is zero point six six three nine,

128
00:08:34,912 --> 00:08:38,800
and so is the cosine of forty
eight point four degrees.

129
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:43,748
So wherever this video
has a sine of the latitude,

130
00:08:43,748 --> 00:08:48,575
a book that uses the colatitude
has a cosine, and the other way round.

131
00:08:49,375 --> 00:08:52,050
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

132
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:55,362
The colatitude is measured
down from the pole,

133
00:08:55,362 --> 00:09:00,255
so a book that uses it has a cosine
wherever we have a sine of the latitude,

134
00:09:00,255 --> 00:09:01,800
and the other way round.

135
00:09:02,150 --> 00:09:04,325
Now for the second angle.

136
00:09:04,675 --> 00:09:08,300
First, we need another
kind of line on the globe.

137
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:12,725
Start at the North Pole, and draw a line
straight down the globe,

138
00:09:12,725 --> 00:09:16,675
heading due south all the way, until
it reaches the South Pole.

139
00:09:17,025 --> 00:09:20,050
This half circle is called a meridian.

140
00:09:20,400 --> 00:09:25,211
All along one meridian, the Sun
is highest in the sky at the same moment,

141
00:09:25,211 --> 00:09:28,375
at midday, and that's
where the name comes from.

142
00:09:28,725 --> 00:09:31,743
Let's turn the globe
and draw more of them,

143
00:09:31,743 --> 00:09:34,550
twelve, evenly spaced all the way round.

144
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:39,825
Every meridian runs from pole
to pole, and they all meet at the poles.

145
00:09:40,175 --> 00:09:44,950
Each one is half of a big circle
whose centre is the Earth's centre.

146
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:49,925
That's the difference from the parallels,
whose centres sit on the axis.

147
00:09:50,725 --> 00:09:54,325
One meridian is chosen
as the starting line.

148
00:09:54,675 --> 00:09:57,162
It passes through Greenwich, in London,

149
00:09:57,162 --> 00:10:01,625
and it was agreed at an international
meeting in eighteen eighty four.

150
00:10:01,975 --> 00:10:04,275
It's called the prime meridian.

151
00:10:04,625 --> 00:10:08,325
Let's follow it from the top
of the globe to the bottom.

152
00:10:08,675 --> 00:10:13,425
It starts at the North Pole, in the middle
of the frozen Arctic Ocean.

153
00:10:13,775 --> 00:10:18,337
It runs south between Greenland
and Norway, over the Norwegian Sea and

154
00:10:18,337 --> 00:10:23,225
the North Sea, and comes ashore on the
east coast of England, in Yorkshire.

155
00:10:23,575 --> 00:10:26,523
It passes through Greenwich,
in east London,

156
00:10:26,523 --> 00:10:30,075
and leaves England on the south
coast, near Brighton.

157
00:10:30,425 --> 00:10:34,239
It crosses the English Channel,
and runs right through France,

158
00:10:34,239 --> 00:10:38,300
from Normandy in the north
to the Pyrenees mountains in the south.

159
00:10:38,650 --> 00:10:40,958
Then it crosses the east of Spain,

160
00:10:40,958 --> 00:10:45,100
and reaches the Mediterranean
Sea a little north of Valencia.

161
00:10:45,450 --> 00:10:51,825
In Africa, it crosses the Sahara desert
in Algeria, then Mali, and Burkina Faso.

162
00:10:52,175 --> 00:10:56,953
Further south, it runs for a short
way along the border between Togo and

163
00:10:56,953 --> 00:11:01,200
Ghana, then through Ghana, to the coast
near the capital, Accra.

164
00:11:01,550 --> 00:11:07,150
Out in the Gulf of Guinea, part of the
Atlantic Ocean, it crosses the equator.

165
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,969
Then it runs south over the open
South Atlantic and

166
00:11:10,969 --> 00:11:15,050
the Southern Ocean, for more
than eight thousand kilometres.

167
00:11:15,400 --> 00:11:19,875
It reaches the ice of Antarctica,
and finally the South Pole.

168
00:11:20,675 --> 00:11:23,450
Now we can define the second angle.

169
00:11:23,800 --> 00:11:27,675
Let's look down on the Earth
from far above the North Pole.

170
00:11:28,025 --> 00:11:31,999
From up here, every meridian
looks like a spoke of a wheel,

171
00:11:31,999 --> 00:11:33,750
running out from the pole.

172
00:11:34,100 --> 00:11:37,413
Now slice the Earth
across, along the equator,

173
00:11:37,413 --> 00:11:40,150
and look at the round face of the cut.

174
00:11:40,500 --> 00:11:42,661
Its centre is the Earth's centre,

175
00:11:42,661 --> 00:11:46,525
and the meridians meet its rim
like the ends of the spokes.

176
00:11:46,875 --> 00:11:49,496
Longitude is the angle at the centre,

177
00:11:49,496 --> 00:11:53,675
from the prime meridian round
to the meridian of the place.

178
00:11:54,025 --> 00:11:56,875
We write it with the Greek letter lambda.

179
00:11:57,225 --> 00:12:01,288
Seen from above the North
Pole, east is anticlockwise,

180
00:12:01,288 --> 00:12:04,750
the same way the angle
theta turned in Part 1.

181
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:08,541
Places east of Greenwich
have positive longitude,

182
00:12:08,541 --> 00:12:11,350
up to a hundred and eighty degrees east.

183
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,856
Places to the west have
negative longitude,

184
00:12:14,856 --> 00:12:17,425
down to minus a hundred and eighty.

185
00:12:17,775 --> 00:12:21,625
Brest is about four and a half
degrees west of Greenwich,

186
00:12:21,625 --> 00:12:24,800
so its lambda is minus
four point five degrees.

187
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:30,567
Some data sets count longitude
from zero to three hundred and

188
00:12:30,567 --> 00:12:32,875
sixty degrees, always going east.

189
00:12:33,225 --> 00:12:37,543
Then Brest is at three hundred
and sixty minus four point five,

190
00:12:37,543 --> 00:12:41,450
which is three hundred and fifty
five point five degrees.

191
00:12:42,950 --> 00:12:45,625
Both numbers point to the same meridian,

192
00:12:45,625 --> 00:12:48,500
so always check which
way your data counts.

193
00:12:49,300 --> 00:12:52,800
Now let's cover the globe
with both kinds of line.

194
00:12:53,150 --> 00:12:57,646
First, the meridians, one every
fifteen degrees of longitude:

195
00:12:57,646 --> 00:13:01,700
zero, fifteen east, thirty
east, and all the way round.

196
00:13:02,050 --> 00:13:06,067
Then the parallels, one every
fifteen degrees of latitude:

197
00:13:06,067 --> 00:13:07,937
the equator, fifteen north,

198
00:13:07,937 --> 00:13:13,200
thirty north, forty five, sixty and
seventy five, and the same to the south.

199
00:13:13,550 --> 00:13:17,775
Together, they make a grid
on the globe, called the graticule.

200
00:13:18,125 --> 00:13:21,819
Every meridian crosses every
parallel at a right angle,

201
00:13:21,819 --> 00:13:27,125
because the meridians run north and south,
and the parallels run east and west.

202
00:13:27,475 --> 00:13:32,025
Any place on Earth sits where
one parallel meets one meridian.

203
00:13:32,375 --> 00:13:36,975
So two numbers, its latitude
and its longitude, pin it down.

204
00:13:37,325 --> 00:13:41,394
Brest sits where the parallel
at forty eight point four degrees

205
00:13:41,394 --> 00:13:45,075
north meets the meridian
at four point five degrees west.

206
00:13:45,875 --> 00:13:50,625
Latitude and longitude tell us
where a place is on the surface.

207
00:13:50,975 --> 00:13:55,285
But in physics, we often want
the place as an arrow in space,

208
00:13:55,285 --> 00:14:01,150
from the Earth's centre, with x, y and z
parts, like the balloon's arrow in Part 1.

209
00:14:01,500 --> 00:14:06,650
For that, we first need three axes, all
starting at the Earth's centre.

210
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,750
The z axis runs up the Earth's
spin axis, towards the North Pole.

211
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:17,132
The x axis runs out to the point where
the prime meridian meets the equator,

212
00:14:17,132 --> 00:14:20,575
in the Gulf of Guinea, off
the coast of West Africa.

213
00:14:20,925 --> 00:14:25,138
The y axis runs out to the equator
at ninety degrees east,

214
00:14:25,138 --> 00:14:28,625
in the Indian Ocean, south
of the Bay of Bengal.

215
00:14:28,975 --> 00:14:34,250
The arrows of length one along them
are called x hat, y hat and z hat.

216
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:37,875
All three are at right
angles to each other.

217
00:14:38,225 --> 00:14:42,285
They're arranged so that x hat
cross y hat gives z hat,

218
00:14:42,285 --> 00:14:44,500
as in the cross product movie.

219
00:14:44,850 --> 00:14:49,675
And they turn with the Earth, so
a city's x, y and z never change.

220
00:14:50,025 --> 00:14:53,850
Our goal now is to find
Brest's x, y and z.

221
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:57,875
We'll do it in two
steps, with one slice each time.

222
00:14:58,675 --> 00:15:02,050
The next few minutes build
Brest's position, and then

223
00:15:02,050 --> 00:15:05,425
the speed of the spinning
ground, one step at a time.

224
00:15:05,775 --> 00:15:08,507
They're here for anyone
who wants every step,

225
00:15:08,507 --> 00:15:13,425
and you don't need them for the intuition
of weather, climate science or mapping.

226
00:15:13,775 --> 00:15:18,050
So if it feels like a lot, skip
ahead to the time on screen.

227
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,575
First, the side view.

228
00:15:20,925 --> 00:15:24,381
Let's slice the Earth along
Brest's meridian again,

229
00:15:24,381 --> 00:15:28,175
and next to it, draw the same
cut face flat on the page.

230
00:15:28,525 --> 00:15:30,623
Everything we draw on the globe,

231
00:15:30,623 --> 00:15:33,900
we'll draw on the flat
diagram at the same moment.

232
00:15:34,250 --> 00:15:37,225
Here's the centre, in both pictures.

233
00:15:37,575 --> 00:15:42,875
Here's the Earth's axis, pointing up
to the North Pole: that's the z direction.

234
00:15:43,225 --> 00:15:48,075
Here's the line from the centre out
to the equator, on Brest's meridian.

235
00:15:48,425 --> 00:15:53,375
Here's the arrow from the centre
to Brest, as long as the Earth's radius.

236
00:15:53,725 --> 00:15:57,888
And here's the latitude,
phi, forty eight point four degrees,

237
00:15:57,888 --> 00:16:00,550
between the equator line and the arrow.

238
00:16:00,900 --> 00:16:05,177
Now drop a line from Brest
straight down to the equator plane,

239
00:16:05,177 --> 00:16:06,625
parallel to the axis.

240
00:16:06,975 --> 00:16:10,175
It meets the equator
line at a right angle.

241
00:16:10,525 --> 00:16:15,275
So we have a right triangle,
and the arrow to Brest is its long side.

242
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:19,125
What's Brest's height
above the equator plane?

243
00:16:19,475 --> 00:16:24,375
That height is the side of the triangle
that runs parallel to the axis,

244
00:16:24,375 --> 00:16:26,100
so it's measured along z.

245
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:29,425
It's the side opposite the angle phi.

246
00:16:29,775 --> 00:16:34,250
By the rule from Part 1, it's
the long side times sine phi.

247
00:16:34,600 --> 00:16:38,225
So z equals the Earth's
radius times sine phi.

248
00:16:38,575 --> 00:16:40,350
Now the numbers.

249
00:16:40,700 --> 00:16:43,240
The long side is the Earth's radius,

250
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:47,050
six thousand three hundred
and seventy one kilometres.

251
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:52,875
The sine of forty eight point four degrees
is zero point seven four seven eight.

252
00:16:53,225 --> 00:16:55,961
So z is six thousand three hundred and

253
00:16:55,961 --> 00:16:59,706
seventy one times
zero point seven four seven eight,

254
00:16:59,706 --> 00:17:04,675
which is about four thousand seven
hundred and sixty four kilometres.

255
00:17:06,175 --> 00:17:07,347
With easy numbers,

256
00:17:07,347 --> 00:17:12,424
six thousand four hundred times three
quarters is four thousand eight hundred:

257
00:17:12,424 --> 00:17:18,341
about the same. So Brest sits about
four thousand seven hundred and

258
00:17:18,341 --> 00:17:23,200
sixty four kilometres above the equator
plane, on the North Pole's side.

259
00:17:23,550 --> 00:17:27,781
A place in the southern hemisphere
has a negative latitude,

260
00:17:27,781 --> 00:17:32,800
so its sine, and its z, are negative:
it lies below the equator plane.

261
00:17:33,600 --> 00:17:39,250
Next, the triangle's other short side:
Brest's distance from the Earth's axis.

262
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:45,075
It's the side next to the angle phi, so
it's the long side times cosine phi.

263
00:17:45,425 --> 00:17:50,975
The cosine of forty eight point four
degrees is zero point six six three nine.

264
00:17:51,325 --> 00:17:55,570
So the distance from the axis
is six thousand three hundred and

265
00:17:55,570 --> 00:17:58,805
seventy one times
zero point six six three nine,

266
00:17:58,805 --> 00:18:03,050
which is about four thousand two
hundred and thirty kilometres.

267
00:18:04,550 --> 00:18:05,857
With easy numbers,

268
00:18:05,857 --> 00:18:11,736
six thousand four hundred times two thirds
is about four thousand two hundred and

269
00:18:11,736 --> 00:18:12,825
seventy: close.

270
00:18:14,325 --> 00:18:18,494
This distance is exactly
the radius of Brest's parallel,

271
00:18:18,494 --> 00:18:21,025
the disc we sliced across earlier.

272
00:18:21,375 --> 00:18:25,374
On both pictures, the two short
sides now carry their lengths:

273
00:18:25,374 --> 00:18:27,373
four thousand seven hundred and

274
00:18:27,373 --> 00:18:31,178
sixty four kilometres up, and four
thousand two hundred and

275
00:18:31,178 --> 00:18:33,500
thirty kilometres out from the axis.

276
00:18:34,300 --> 00:18:36,300
Now for the top view.

277
00:18:36,650 --> 00:18:40,450
Now look down on the Earth
from far above the North Pole,

278
00:18:40,450 --> 00:18:45,050
and slice it across along the equator
plane, as we did for longitude.

279
00:18:45,400 --> 00:18:49,175
Next to it, again, the same
face is drawn flat.

280
00:18:49,525 --> 00:18:53,445
Brest itself is above this
plane, but straight below Brest,

281
00:18:53,445 --> 00:18:56,700
in the plane, is the foot
of the line we dropped.

282
00:18:57,050 --> 00:18:59,835
That foot is four thousand two hundred and

283
00:18:59,835 --> 00:19:04,875
thirty kilometres from the centre, the
distance from the axis we just found.

284
00:19:05,225 --> 00:19:09,518
And it lies on Brest's meridian,
so it's at Brest's longitude,

285
00:19:09,518 --> 00:19:13,950
lambda, minus four and a half degrees,
measured from the x axis.

286
00:19:14,300 --> 00:19:18,616
That's polar coordinates on a flat
face, exactly as in Part 1,

287
00:19:18,616 --> 00:19:22,375
with the distance from the axis
playing the part of r,

288
00:19:22,375 --> 00:19:24,950
and lambda playing the part of theta.

289
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:28,974
So the x part is that
distance times cosine lambda,

290
00:19:28,974 --> 00:19:32,575
and the y part is that
distance times sine lambda.

291
00:19:32,925 --> 00:19:34,700
Now the numbers.

292
00:19:35,050 --> 00:19:40,775
The cosine of minus four point five
degrees is zero point nine nine six nine.

293
00:19:41,125 --> 00:19:43,839
So x is four thousand two hundred and

294
00:19:43,839 --> 00:19:46,994
thirty times
zero point nine nine six nine,

295
00:19:46,994 --> 00:19:51,175
about four thousand two hundred
and seventeen kilometres.

296
00:19:52,675 --> 00:19:58,825
The sine of minus four point five degrees
is minus zero point zero seven eight five.

297
00:19:59,175 --> 00:20:01,812
So y is four thousand two hundred and

298
00:20:01,812 --> 00:20:05,519
thirty times minus
zero point zero seven eight five,

299
00:20:05,519 --> 00:20:09,225
about minus three hundred
and thirty two kilometres.

300
00:20:10,725 --> 00:20:15,186
The y part is small, because Brest
is close to the prime meridian,

301
00:20:15,186 --> 00:20:18,700
and it's negative, because
Brest is just west of it.

302
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:22,000
Let's put the two steps together.

303
00:20:22,350 --> 00:20:27,275
The distance from the axis
is the Earth's radius times cosine phi.

304
00:20:27,625 --> 00:20:33,175
So x is the Earth's radius, times cosine
phi, times cosine lambda.

305
00:20:33,525 --> 00:20:38,800
And y is the Earth's radius, times cosine
phi, times sine lambda.

306
00:20:39,150 --> 00:20:42,750
And z is the Earth's
radius times sine phi.

307
00:20:43,100 --> 00:20:46,150
For Brest, that's four
thousand two hundred and

308
00:20:46,150 --> 00:20:48,357
seventeen, minus three hundred and

309
00:20:48,357 --> 00:20:52,900
thirty two, and four thousand seven
hundred and sixty four kilometres.

310
00:20:54,400 --> 00:20:58,950
Let's check that this arrow really
is as long as the Earth's radius.

311
00:20:59,300 --> 00:21:01,989
First, Pythagoras in the equator plane:

312
00:21:01,989 --> 00:21:04,954
the square root
of x squared plus y squared

313
00:21:04,954 --> 00:21:07,574
gives back the distance from the axis,

314
00:21:07,574 --> 00:21:10,125
four thousand two hundred and thirty.

315
00:21:11,625 --> 00:21:13,820
Then Pythagoras in the side view:

316
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:17,012
the square root of four
thousand two hundred and

317
00:21:17,012 --> 00:21:22,400
thirty squared, plus z squared, gives six
thousand three hundred and seventy one.

318
00:21:23,900 --> 00:21:29,600
It works for every place, because cosine
squared plus sine squared is always one.

319
00:21:29,950 --> 00:21:33,950
Now divide the arrow by its
length, the Earth's radius.

320
00:21:34,300 --> 00:21:36,252
We get an arrow of length one,

321
00:21:36,252 --> 00:21:40,025
pointing straight up from the
place, away from the centre.

322
00:21:40,375 --> 00:21:43,800
We call it r hat, just as on the flat map.

323
00:21:44,150 --> 00:21:50,175
Its parts are cosine phi cosine lambda,
cosine phi sine lambda, and sine phi.

324
00:21:50,525 --> 00:21:54,007
For Brest, they're about
zero point six six two,

325
00:21:54,007 --> 00:21:58,650
minus zero point zero five two, and
zero point seven four eight.

326
00:22:00,150 --> 00:22:05,675
We'll use this arrow again in Part 4, as
the up direction of a weather station.

327
00:22:06,475 --> 00:22:10,200
Let's do the same for a city
on the other side of Asia.

328
00:22:10,550 --> 00:22:13,438
Khulna is in the southwest of Bangladesh,

329
00:22:13,438 --> 00:22:17,452
near the Sundarbans,
the world's largest mangrove forest,

330
00:22:17,452 --> 00:22:22,875
close to where Cyclone Amphan, from
Part 2, came ashore in May twenty twenty.

331
00:22:23,225 --> 00:22:26,455
It's at about twenty
two point eight degrees north,

332
00:22:26,455 --> 00:22:28,925
and eighty nine point six degrees east.

333
00:22:29,275 --> 00:22:33,900
In the side view, sine phi
is zero point three eight seven five.

334
00:22:34,250 --> 00:22:39,306
So Khulna's height above the equator
plane is six thousand three hundred and

335
00:22:39,306 --> 00:22:42,766
seventy one times
zero point three eight seven five,

336
00:22:42,766 --> 00:22:46,625
about two thousand four hundred
and sixty nine kilometres.

337
00:22:48,125 --> 00:22:51,155
Cosine phi
is zero point nine two one nine,

338
00:22:51,155 --> 00:22:56,088
so its distance from the axis is about
five thousand eight hundred and

339
00:22:56,088 --> 00:22:57,850
seventy three kilometres.

340
00:22:59,350 --> 00:23:02,715
Khulna is much closer
to the equator than Brest,

341
00:23:02,715 --> 00:23:06,150
so it sits lower, and much
farther from the axis.

342
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:10,593
In the top view, lambda
is eighty nine point six degrees,

343
00:23:10,593 --> 00:23:13,825
almost a right angle
from the prime meridian.

344
00:23:14,175 --> 00:23:18,483
Its cosine is only
zero point zero zero six nine eight,

345
00:23:18,483 --> 00:23:21,225
so x is about forty one kilometres.

346
00:23:22,725 --> 00:23:24,936
Its sine is almost exactly one,

347
00:23:24,936 --> 00:23:30,000
so y is about five thousand eight
hundred and seventy three kilometres.

348
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:35,372
So Khulna's arrow points almost
straight along the y axis,

349
00:23:35,372 --> 00:23:37,575
tilted up towards the North Pole.

350
00:23:37,925 --> 00:23:42,686
And once again, its length comes out
at six thousand three hundred and

351
00:23:42,686 --> 00:23:44,250
seventy one kilometres.

352
00:23:45,050 --> 00:23:48,800
That distance from the axis
has a second meaning.

353
00:23:49,150 --> 00:23:51,359
The Earth spins round its axis,

354
00:23:51,359 --> 00:23:56,275
so every place on the ground is carried
round a circle: its parallel.

355
00:23:56,625 --> 00:24:00,658
The radius of that circle
is the distance from the axis,

356
00:24:00,658 --> 00:24:03,250
the Earth's radius times cosine phi.

357
00:24:03,600 --> 00:24:06,000
How fast is the ground moving?

358
00:24:06,350 --> 00:24:09,375
Let's work it out, one step at a time.

359
00:24:09,725 --> 00:24:12,600
First, how long does one turn take?

360
00:24:12,950 --> 00:24:15,358
Measured against the distant stars,

361
00:24:15,358 --> 00:24:21,000
the Earth turns once every twenty three
hours, fifty six minutes and four seconds.

362
00:24:21,350 --> 00:24:23,850
We call this time capital T.

363
00:24:24,200 --> 00:24:27,710
It's four minutes shorter
than a day from noon to noon,

364
00:24:27,710 --> 00:24:31,475
because the Earth also moves
along its orbit round the Sun.

365
00:24:31,825 --> 00:24:35,450
Next, how far does the place
travel in one turn?

366
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:40,700
Once round its circle, which is two
pi times the radius of the circle.

367
00:24:41,050 --> 00:24:45,575
That's two pi, times the Earth's
radius, times cosine phi.

368
00:24:45,925 --> 00:24:49,050
So, speed is distance divided by time.

369
00:24:49,400 --> 00:24:52,717
So the ground speed, capital U, is two pi,

370
00:24:52,717 --> 00:24:57,850
times the Earth's radius, times cosine
phi, divided by capital T.

371
00:24:58,200 --> 00:25:02,621
Fourth, we pull out the part
that's the same for every place:

372
00:25:02,621 --> 00:25:04,650
two pi divided by capital T.

373
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:09,400
That's the angle the Earth turns
through each second, in radians.

374
00:25:09,750 --> 00:25:12,067
It's called the Earth's spin rate,

375
00:25:12,067 --> 00:25:16,225
and we write it capital
Omega, as in the cross product movie.

376
00:25:16,575 --> 00:25:22,875
So capital U equals capital Omega, times
the Earth's radius, times cosine phi.

377
00:25:24,375 --> 00:25:26,923
It's Part 2's rule for a ring again:

378
00:25:26,923 --> 00:25:30,250
the speed is the radius
times the turning rate.

379
00:25:31,050 --> 00:25:33,725
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

380
00:25:34,075 --> 00:25:36,996
A place at latitude phi sits the Earth's

381
00:25:36,996 --> 00:25:40,356
radius times sine phi
above the equator plane,

382
00:25:40,356 --> 00:25:44,300
and the radius times cosine
phi from the Earth's axis.

383
00:25:44,650 --> 00:25:49,351
As the Earth spins, that place
is carried east at capital Omega,

384
00:25:49,351 --> 00:25:51,775
times its distance from the axis.

385
00:25:52,125 --> 00:25:53,900
Now the numbers.

386
00:25:54,250 --> 00:25:57,115
Twenty three hours, fifty six minutes and

387
00:25:57,115 --> 00:26:02,075
four seconds is eighty six thousand
one hundred and sixty four seconds.

388
00:26:02,425 --> 00:26:07,664
So capital Omega is two pi divided by
eighty six thousand one hundred and

389
00:26:07,664 --> 00:26:11,683
sixty four, which is about
seven point two nine two times

390
00:26:11,683 --> 00:26:14,625
ten to the minus five radians per second.

391
00:26:16,125 --> 00:26:19,200
At the equator, cosine phi is one.

392
00:26:19,550 --> 00:26:24,751
So the ground moves at seven point two
nine two times ten to the minus five,

393
00:26:24,751 --> 00:26:27,967
times six point three seven one
million metres,

394
00:26:27,967 --> 00:26:32,825
which is about four hundred and sixty
four point six metres per second.

395
00:26:34,325 --> 00:26:39,176
With easy numbers, the equator is about
forty thousand kilometres round,

396
00:26:39,176 --> 00:26:42,141
and forty thousand
kilometres in twenty four

397
00:26:42,141 --> 00:26:45,038
hours is about one
thousand six hundred and

398
00:26:45,038 --> 00:26:46,925
seventy kilometres per hour.

399
00:26:47,275 --> 00:26:50,975
That's almost twice
as fast as a passenger jet.

400
00:26:51,325 --> 00:26:53,598
Singapore, almost on the equator,

401
00:26:53,598 --> 00:26:57,800
moves at about four hundred
and sixty four metres per second.

402
00:26:59,300 --> 00:27:03,143
At Khulna, cosine phi
is zero point nine two one nine,

403
00:27:03,143 --> 00:27:06,275
so it's about four
hundred and twenty eight.

404
00:27:07,775 --> 00:27:11,605
At Brest, cosine phi
is zero point six six three nine,

405
00:27:11,605 --> 00:27:14,300
so it's about three hundred and eight.

406
00:27:15,800 --> 00:27:19,510
At Longyearbyen,
on the Arctic islands of Svalbard,

407
00:27:19,510 --> 00:27:22,274
seventy eight point two degrees north,

408
00:27:22,274 --> 00:27:25,475
cosine phi
is zero point two zero four five.

409
00:27:25,825 --> 00:27:30,600
So the ground there moves at only
about ninety five metres per second.

410
00:27:32,100 --> 00:27:37,525
And at the poles, cosine phi is zero, so
the ground just turns on the spot.

411
00:27:37,875 --> 00:27:42,175
Every one of these speeds points
east, the way the Earth turns.

412
00:27:42,525 --> 00:27:45,981
We don't feel any of it,
because the air, the sea and

413
00:27:45,981 --> 00:27:48,850
we ourselves all move
along with the ground.

414
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:53,307
But this shrinking circle is part
of the reason why the Earth's

415
00:27:53,307 --> 00:27:57,088
spin affects the winds differently
at different latitudes,

416
00:27:57,088 --> 00:27:59,500
as we saw in the cross product movie.

417
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:04,550
Now let's measure distances
on the ground, step by step.

418
00:28:04,900 --> 00:28:09,572
If you'd rather keep to the big picture,
skip ahead to the time on screen:

419
00:28:09,572 --> 00:28:13,045
these steps aren't needed
for the intuition of weather,

420
00:28:13,045 --> 00:28:14,750
climate science or mapping.

421
00:28:15,100 --> 00:28:17,300
Let's start with going north.

422
00:28:17,650 --> 00:28:20,650
Slice the Earth along a meridian again.

423
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,749
Walking due north, you
follow the meridian,

424
00:28:23,749 --> 00:28:28,225
and a meridian is part of a circle
whose centre is the Earth's centre.

425
00:28:28,575 --> 00:28:33,025
So the radius of that circle
is the full radius of the Earth.

426
00:28:33,375 --> 00:28:36,147
As you walk north, your latitude grows,

427
00:28:36,147 --> 00:28:40,625
and latitude is exactly the angle
at the centre of this circle.

428
00:28:40,975 --> 00:28:43,739
So by the arc length rule from Part 1,

429
00:28:43,739 --> 00:28:48,975
the distance walked is the radius
times the change of angle, in radians.

430
00:28:49,325 --> 00:28:53,074
We write it like this:
delta y, the distance north,

431
00:28:53,074 --> 00:28:57,925
equals the Earth's radius times delta
phi, the change in latitude.

432
00:28:58,275 --> 00:29:00,875
Let's find the length of one degree.

433
00:29:01,225 --> 00:29:06,225
A full turn is three hundred
and sixty degrees, or two pi radians.

434
00:29:06,575 --> 00:29:10,104
So one degree is pi over one
hundred and eighty,

435
00:29:10,104 --> 00:29:14,000
which is zero point zero
one seven four five radians.

436
00:29:15,500 --> 00:29:19,513
Times six thousand three hundred
and seventy one kilometres,

437
00:29:19,513 --> 00:29:23,325
that's about one hundred
and eleven point two kilometres.

438
00:29:24,825 --> 00:29:26,153
With easy numbers,

439
00:29:26,153 --> 00:29:32,128
six thousand four hundred times zero point
zero one seven five is one hundred and

440
00:29:32,128 --> 00:29:33,825
twelve: about the same.

441
00:29:35,325 --> 00:29:37,922
And it's the same at every latitude,

442
00:29:37,922 --> 00:29:41,025
because every meridian
has the same radius.

443
00:29:41,375 --> 00:29:46,225
So a degree north is always
about a hundred and eleven kilometres.

444
00:29:47,025 --> 00:29:48,800
Two examples.

445
00:29:49,150 --> 00:29:54,095
One minute of latitude is a sixtieth
of a degree, so it's one hundred and

446
00:29:54,095 --> 00:29:59,650
eleven point two divided by sixty, which
is about one point eight five kilometres.

447
00:30:01,150 --> 00:30:04,572
That's the nautical mile
from the weather basics movie,

448
00:30:04,572 --> 00:30:06,500
and that's where it comes from.

449
00:30:06,850 --> 00:30:12,550
Next, how far is Brest from the equator,
walking due south along its meridian?

450
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:16,294
Forty eight point four
degrees, times one hundred and

451
00:30:16,294 --> 00:30:18,791
eleven point two kilometres per degree,

452
00:30:18,791 --> 00:30:22,825
is about five thousand three
hundred and eighty two kilometres.

453
00:30:24,325 --> 00:30:28,736
Notice that's longer than Brest's
height above the equator plane,

454
00:30:28,736 --> 00:30:32,400
four thousand seven hundred
and sixty four kilometres.

455
00:30:32,750 --> 00:30:35,875
The walk follows the curved
surface of the Earth.

456
00:30:36,225 --> 00:30:39,650
The height is a straight
line, through the inside.

457
00:30:40,450 --> 00:30:42,600
Going east is different.

458
00:30:42,950 --> 00:30:46,075
Walking due east, you follow a parallel.

459
00:30:46,425 --> 00:30:49,888
And a parallel, as we saw
when we sliced across,

460
00:30:49,888 --> 00:30:54,000
is a circle round the Earth's
axis, not round its centre.

461
00:30:54,350 --> 00:31:00,325
Its radius is the distance from the axis:
the Earth's radius times cosine phi.

462
00:31:00,675 --> 00:31:04,000
Let's slice across
at Brest's latitude again.

463
00:31:04,350 --> 00:31:08,297
As you walk east along this
circle, your longitude grows,

464
00:31:08,297 --> 00:31:13,075
and longitude is the angle at the centre
of this circle, on the axis.

465
00:31:13,425 --> 00:31:15,586
So by the same arc length rule,

466
00:31:15,586 --> 00:31:21,025
the distance east is the radius of the
parallel times the change in longitude.

467
00:31:21,375 --> 00:31:25,802
We write it like this:
delta x equals the Earth's radius,

468
00:31:25,802 --> 00:31:28,675
times cosine phi, times delta lambda.

469
00:31:29,025 --> 00:31:33,071
For one degree at Brest, that's
four thousand two hundred and

470
00:31:33,071 --> 00:31:37,051
thirty kilometres times
zero point zero one seven four five,

471
00:31:37,051 --> 00:31:40,500
which is about seventy
three point eight kilometres.

472
00:31:42,000 --> 00:31:44,586
That's only two thirds of a degree north,

473
00:31:44,586 --> 00:31:48,750
because Brest's parallel is only
two thirds as big as the equator.

474
00:31:49,550 --> 00:31:52,225
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

475
00:31:52,575 --> 00:31:57,217
A degree of latitude is always
about a hundred and eleven kilometres,

476
00:31:57,217 --> 00:32:00,647
but a degree of longitude
is that times cosine phi,

477
00:32:00,647 --> 00:32:02,800
so it shrinks towards the poles.

478
00:32:03,150 --> 00:32:08,050
Let's compare one degree of longitude
at four places, side by side.

479
00:32:08,400 --> 00:32:14,050
Each one is one hundred and eleven
point two kilometres, times cosine phi.

480
00:32:14,400 --> 00:32:17,772
At Singapore, cosine phi
is almost exactly one,

481
00:32:17,772 --> 00:32:20,857
so one degree east
is about one hundred and

482
00:32:20,857 --> 00:32:24,875
eleven point two kilometres,
the same as a degree north.

483
00:32:26,375 --> 00:32:30,330
At Khulna, cosine phi
is zero point nine two one nine,

484
00:32:30,330 --> 00:32:33,625
so it's about one hundred
and two point five.

485
00:32:35,125 --> 00:32:38,400
At Brest, it's about
seventy three point eight.

486
00:32:39,900 --> 00:32:44,368
At Longyearbyen, cosine phi
is zero point two zero four five,

487
00:32:44,368 --> 00:32:49,275
so one degree east is only about
twenty two point seven kilometres.

488
00:32:50,775 --> 00:32:54,675
And at the pole, where all
the meridians meet, it's zero.

489
00:32:55,025 --> 00:32:57,380
Like the segments of a peeled orange,

490
00:32:57,380 --> 00:33:00,308
the meridians are widest
apart at the equator,

491
00:33:00,308 --> 00:33:02,600
and they come together at the poles.

492
00:33:02,950 --> 00:33:06,680
Unlike an orange, which
has a fixed number of segments,

493
00:33:06,680 --> 00:33:09,800
there's a meridian through
every single place.

494
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:14,275
This matters every time
we use weather data.

495
00:33:14,625 --> 00:33:18,463
ERA5, the reanalysis we've
used throughout this series,

496
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:22,022
is a reconstruction
of the past weather everywhere,

497
00:33:22,022 --> 00:33:25,650
made by combining observations
with a weather model.

498
00:33:26,000 --> 00:33:31,875
It gives its values on a grid of latitude
and longitude, every quarter of a degree.

499
00:33:32,225 --> 00:33:37,450
So each grid box is a quarter of a degree
tall, and a quarter of a degree wide.

500
00:33:37,800 --> 00:33:41,093
The height is easy:
a quarter of one hundred and

501
00:33:41,093 --> 00:33:46,650
eleven point two is about twenty seven
point eight kilometres, at every latitude.

502
00:33:48,150 --> 00:33:50,900
The width depends on cosine phi.

503
00:33:51,250 --> 00:33:52,186
At Singapore,

504
00:33:52,186 --> 00:33:57,300
a box is about twenty seven point eight
kilometres wide: nearly square.

505
00:33:58,800 --> 00:34:03,275
At Khulna, it's about twenty
five point six kilometres wide.

506
00:34:04,775 --> 00:34:07,625
At Brest, about eighteen and a half.

507
00:34:09,125 --> 00:34:14,401
At Longyearbyen, a box is still twenty
seven point eight kilometres tall,

508
00:34:14,401 --> 00:34:17,075
but only about five point seven wide.

509
00:34:18,575 --> 00:34:22,250
So the boxes get thinner
and thinner towards the poles.

510
00:34:22,600 --> 00:34:23,912
Forget the cosine,

511
00:34:23,912 --> 00:34:28,139
and a box at Longyearbyen comes
out four point nine times,

512
00:34:28,139 --> 00:34:30,325
so about five times, too wide.

513
00:34:31,825 --> 00:34:35,986
Every east-west distance there
would be five times too big,

514
00:34:35,986 --> 00:34:39,300
and every east-west slope
five times too small.

515
00:34:39,650 --> 00:34:41,804
This is also how, in Part 4,

516
00:34:41,804 --> 00:34:46,575
we'll turn a coast's latitudes
and longitudes into kilometres.

517
00:34:46,925 --> 00:34:51,898
And it's one reason why many modern
weather models use other kinds of grid,

518
00:34:51,898 --> 00:34:54,550
which don't crowd together at the poles.

519
00:34:55,350 --> 00:34:57,925
Distances give us areas.

520
00:34:58,275 --> 00:34:59,900
Why do we care?

521
00:35:00,250 --> 00:35:04,551
Because to average anything over
the Earth, like the temperature,

522
00:35:04,551 --> 00:35:07,925
we need to know how much
area each grid box covers.

523
00:35:08,275 --> 00:35:11,781
The next few minutes build
the area of a small patch,

524
00:35:11,781 --> 00:35:15,750
and then of whole bands round
the Earth, one step at a time.

525
00:35:16,100 --> 00:35:18,634
They're for anyone who wants every step,

526
00:35:18,634 --> 00:35:21,422
and not needed
for the intuition of weather,

527
00:35:21,422 --> 00:35:26,300
climate science or mapping, so feel free
to skip ahead to the time on screen.

528
00:35:26,650 --> 00:35:31,900
Take a small patch of the surface,
between two meridians and two parallels.

529
00:35:32,250 --> 00:35:36,825
Its north-south side is the Earth's
radius times delta phi.

530
00:35:37,175 --> 00:35:43,225
Its east-west side is the Earth's radius
times cosine phi times delta lambda.

531
00:35:43,575 --> 00:35:46,034
The two sides meet at a right angle,

532
00:35:46,034 --> 00:35:49,450
because meridians cross
parallels at right angles.

533
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:54,950
And when the patch is small, it's almost
flat, so it's almost a rectangle.

534
00:35:55,300 --> 00:35:59,375
The area of a rectangle
is its width times its height.

535
00:35:59,725 --> 00:36:04,675
So the patch's area is about the east-west
side times the north-south side.

536
00:36:05,025 --> 00:36:08,581
Collecting the pieces, that's
the radius squared,

537
00:36:08,581 --> 00:36:12,500
times cosine phi, times delta
phi, times delta lambda.

538
00:36:12,850 --> 00:36:17,775
As the patch shrinks, the bending
of the surface matters less and less.

539
00:36:18,125 --> 00:36:22,221
In the limit, we write
the tiny area as d capital A,

540
00:36:22,221 --> 00:36:25,450
and the tiny steps as d phi and d lambda.

541
00:36:25,800 --> 00:36:32,400
So d capital A equals the radius squared,
times cosine phi, d phi, d lambda.

542
00:36:32,750 --> 00:36:37,863
From here on, delta means a small
step, and a small d means a step so

543
00:36:37,863 --> 00:36:40,975
tiny that the approximation becomes exact.

544
00:36:41,325 --> 00:36:44,200
And the angles are always in radians.

545
00:36:45,000 --> 00:36:48,725
Let's work out the area of one ERA5 box.

546
00:36:49,075 --> 00:36:54,525
A quarter of a degree is zero point zero
zero four three six radians.

547
00:36:54,875 --> 00:36:58,420
The radius squared is six
thousand three hundred and

548
00:36:58,420 --> 00:37:03,125
seventy one squared, about
forty point six million square kilometres.

549
00:37:04,625 --> 00:37:07,538
Times zero point zero zero four three six,

550
00:37:07,538 --> 00:37:11,146
and times zero point zero
zero four three six again,

551
00:37:11,146 --> 00:37:13,227
that's about seven hundred and

552
00:37:13,227 --> 00:37:17,250
seventy three square kilometres,
for a box on the equator.

553
00:37:18,750 --> 00:37:22,325
Every other box is that, times cosine phi.

554
00:37:22,675 --> 00:37:24,671
At Khulna, seven hundred and

555
00:37:24,671 --> 00:37:30,018
seventy three times zero point nine
two one nine is about seven hundred and

556
00:37:30,018 --> 00:37:31,800
twelve square kilometres.

557
00:37:33,300 --> 00:37:36,611
At Brest, times
zero point six six three nine,

558
00:37:36,611 --> 00:37:39,275
it's about five hundred and thirteen.

559
00:37:40,775 --> 00:37:44,513
At Longyearbyen, times
zero point two zero four five,

560
00:37:44,513 --> 00:37:47,475
it's only about a hundred and fifty eight.

561
00:37:48,975 --> 00:37:54,700
As a check, a computer can work out the
exact area of the curved patch at Brest.

562
00:37:55,050 --> 00:37:59,881
It's also about five hundred
and thirteen point one square kilometres,

563
00:37:59,881 --> 00:38:03,400
so the flat rectangle
is a very good approximation.

564
00:38:04,900 --> 00:38:06,900
Now a bigger question.

565
00:38:07,250 --> 00:38:11,150
How much of the Earth's surface
lies between the equator and

566
00:38:11,150 --> 00:38:13,750
some latitude, say thirty degrees north?

567
00:38:14,100 --> 00:38:15,525
Why care?

568
00:38:15,875 --> 00:38:19,854
Because that's how much of the planet
is tropical and subtropical,

569
00:38:19,854 --> 00:38:24,375
and it tells us how much each part
of the world counts in a global average.

570
00:38:24,725 --> 00:38:27,675
Let's build that area from thin strips.

571
00:38:28,025 --> 00:38:31,628
Take one strip that goes
all the way round the Earth,

572
00:38:31,628 --> 00:38:36,250
between latitude phi and a little
further north, phi plus delta phi.

573
00:38:36,600 --> 00:38:41,225
Cut it and unroll it, and it's
almost a long, thin rectangle.

574
00:38:41,575 --> 00:38:46,263
Its length is the length of the parallel:
two pi times its radius,

575
00:38:46,263 --> 00:38:50,525
which is two pi, times the Earth's
radius, times cosine phi.

576
00:38:50,875 --> 00:38:55,775
Its width is the step north:
the Earth's radius times delta phi.

577
00:38:56,125 --> 00:38:58,862
So the strip's area is about two pi,

578
00:38:58,862 --> 00:39:03,425
times the radius squared,
times cosine phi, times delta phi.

579
00:39:04,225 --> 00:39:07,525
Adding up lots of cosines
like this looks hard.

580
00:39:07,875 --> 00:39:10,525
But Part 2 gave us a shortcut.

581
00:39:10,875 --> 00:39:13,565
When an angle grows by a small step,

582
00:39:13,565 --> 00:39:17,375
its sine grows by about
its cosine times that step.

583
00:39:17,725 --> 00:39:23,700
So cosine phi times delta phi is just
how much sine phi grows across the strip.

584
00:39:24,050 --> 00:39:26,550
We call that delta sine phi.

585
00:39:26,900 --> 00:39:32,275
Let's check it with a strip ten degrees
wide, from twenty to thirty degrees north.

586
00:39:32,625 --> 00:39:37,200
Ten degrees
is zero point one seven four five radians.

587
00:39:37,550 --> 00:39:40,316
Cosine phi at the middle of the strip,

588
00:39:40,316 --> 00:39:44,100
at twenty five degrees,
is zero point nine zero six.

589
00:39:44,450 --> 00:39:48,317
So cosine phi times delta
phi is zero point nine

590
00:39:48,317 --> 00:39:52,022
zero six times
zero point one seven four five,

591
00:39:52,022 --> 00:39:54,600
about zero point one five eight.

592
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:59,885
And sine phi grows
from zero point three four two,

593
00:39:59,885 --> 00:40:04,200
at twenty degrees,
to zero point five, at thirty degrees.

594
00:40:04,550 --> 00:40:07,850
That's a step
of zero point one five eight too.

595
00:40:09,350 --> 00:40:13,455
So the strip's area is two
pi, times the radius squared,

596
00:40:13,455 --> 00:40:18,000
times delta sine phi: the change
in sine phi across the strip.

597
00:40:18,800 --> 00:40:23,775
Now stack the strips, from the equator
all the way up to latitude phi.

598
00:40:24,125 --> 00:40:29,200
Each strip adds its own step in sine
phi, like the steps of a staircase.

599
00:40:29,550 --> 00:40:33,426
And all the steps of a staircase
add up to the height of the top,

600
00:40:33,426 --> 00:40:35,275
minus the height of the bottom.

601
00:40:35,625 --> 00:40:38,625
At the equator, sine phi is zero.

602
00:40:38,975 --> 00:40:42,500
So all the steps together
add up to just sine phi.

603
00:40:42,850 --> 00:40:45,946
That means the area
between the equator and

604
00:40:45,946 --> 00:40:50,625
latitude phi is two pi, times the radius
squared, times sine phi.

605
00:40:52,125 --> 00:40:56,850
Let's check with ten degree strips, from
the equator to thirty degrees.

606
00:40:57,200 --> 00:41:01,164
Their steps in sine phi are
zero point one seven four,

607
00:41:01,164 --> 00:41:05,275
zero point one six eight, and
zero point one five eight.

608
00:41:06,775 --> 00:41:12,325
They add up to zero point five, which
is exactly the sine of thirty degrees.

609
00:41:13,825 --> 00:41:18,450
This adding up of thinner and thinner
strips has a name: integration.

610
00:41:18,800 --> 00:41:24,025
We write it with a tall S, for
sum, and a small d for the tiny steps.

611
00:41:24,375 --> 00:41:28,140
Inside, we call the latitude
of each strip phi prime,

612
00:41:28,140 --> 00:41:31,550
so it isn't mixed up
with the top latitude, phi.

613
00:41:31,900 --> 00:41:34,830
So the integral of cosine phi prime,

614
00:41:34,830 --> 00:41:38,900
d phi prime, from zero up
to phi, equals sine phi.

615
00:41:39,700 --> 00:41:41,850
Let's check the formula.

616
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:44,725
Go all the way up to the North Pole.

617
00:41:45,075 --> 00:41:47,428
The sine of ninety degrees is one,

618
00:41:47,428 --> 00:41:52,550
so the northern hemisphere has an area
of two pi times the radius squared.

619
00:41:52,900 --> 00:41:57,625
That's about two hundred and fifty
five million square kilometres.

620
00:41:59,125 --> 00:42:03,569
The whole Earth is twice that, four
pi times the radius squared:

621
00:42:03,569 --> 00:42:07,250
about five hundred and ten
million square kilometres.

622
00:42:08,750 --> 00:42:13,044
So the share of the northern
hemisphere between the equator and

623
00:42:13,044 --> 00:42:15,225
latitude phi is simply sine phi.

624
00:42:15,575 --> 00:42:18,500
The sine of thirty degrees is one half.

625
00:42:18,850 --> 00:42:23,200
So half of the northern hemisphere
lies between the equator and

626
00:42:23,200 --> 00:42:24,650
thirty degrees north.

627
00:42:26,150 --> 00:42:28,425
The same goes for the south.

628
00:42:28,775 --> 00:42:33,142
So half of the whole Earth lies
between thirty degrees south and

629
00:42:33,142 --> 00:42:34,575
thirty degrees north.

630
00:42:36,075 --> 00:42:40,675
At Brest's latitude, sine phi
is zero point seven four seven eight,

631
00:42:40,675 --> 00:42:45,550
so about three quarters of the northern
hemisphere lies south of Brest.

632
00:42:47,050 --> 00:42:51,775
The tropics and subtropics are
huge, and the polar caps are small.

633
00:42:52,575 --> 00:42:55,295
There's a lovely picture of this result,

634
00:42:55,295 --> 00:42:58,900
found by Archimedes more
than two thousand years ago.

635
00:42:59,250 --> 00:43:03,966
Wrap the Earth in a cylinder that
just touches it all round the equator,

636
00:43:03,966 --> 00:43:07,175
so the cylinder has the same
radius as the Earth.

637
00:43:07,525 --> 00:43:11,381
Now take our band, from the
equator up to latitude phi,

638
00:43:11,381 --> 00:43:16,500
and push it straight out sideways,
away from the axis, onto the cylinder.

639
00:43:16,850 --> 00:43:17,939
On the cylinder,

640
00:43:17,939 --> 00:43:22,500
it covers a band whose height
is the Earth's radius times sine phi.

641
00:43:22,850 --> 00:43:27,050
That's z, the height above
the equator plane, from earlier.

642
00:43:27,400 --> 00:43:30,950
A cylinder band is a rectangle rolled up.

643
00:43:31,300 --> 00:43:37,200
Its length is two pi times the radius, and
its height is the radius times sine phi.

644
00:43:37,550 --> 00:43:41,233
So its area is two
pi, times the radius squared,

645
00:43:41,233 --> 00:43:45,300
times sine phi: exactly
the same as the Earth's band.

646
00:43:46,800 --> 00:43:48,550
Why do they match?

647
00:43:48,900 --> 00:43:54,136
Near the pole, a strip on the Earth
is shorter round than on the cylinder,

648
00:43:54,136 --> 00:43:55,975
by a factor of cosine phi.

649
00:43:56,325 --> 00:43:58,818
But the Earth's surface there is steep,

650
00:43:58,818 --> 00:44:03,675
so the strip is wider along the surface
than its height, by the same factor.

651
00:44:04,025 --> 00:44:06,775
The two effects cancel exactly.

652
00:44:07,125 --> 00:44:12,350
So slices of the Earth of equal height
have equal areas, wherever they are.

653
00:44:13,150 --> 00:44:15,825
If you skipped, here's what to keep.

654
00:44:16,175 --> 00:44:19,594
Grid boxes shrink towards
the poles by cosine phi,

655
00:44:19,594 --> 00:44:23,218
so the tropics are huge
and the polar caps are small:

656
00:44:23,218 --> 00:44:27,389
half of the whole Earth lies
between thirty degrees south and

657
00:44:27,389 --> 00:44:28,825
thirty degrees north.

658
00:44:29,175 --> 00:44:31,775
Here's where this matters for climate.

659
00:44:32,125 --> 00:44:37,300
Imagine a planet with just two grid
boxes, the same size in degrees.

660
00:44:37,650 --> 00:44:41,375
One is on the equator,
at thirty degrees Celsius.

661
00:44:41,725 --> 00:44:46,375
The other is at sixty degrees
north, at zero degrees Celsius.

662
00:44:46,725 --> 00:44:49,600
A plain average says fifteen degrees.

663
00:44:49,950 --> 00:44:54,446
But the box at sixty degrees
covers only cosine sixty degrees,

664
00:44:54,446 --> 00:44:58,000
which is one half of the area
of the equator box.

665
00:44:58,350 --> 00:45:01,990
So the area-weighted
average is thirty times one,

666
00:45:01,990 --> 00:45:06,150
plus zero times one half, all
divided by one and a half.

667
00:45:06,500 --> 00:45:08,550
That's twenty degrees.

668
00:45:10,050 --> 00:45:15,175
That's why a global average gives
each latitude a weight of cosine phi.

669
00:45:15,525 --> 00:45:21,454
Now let's do it with real data: ERA5's air
temperature two metres above the ground,

670
00:45:21,454 --> 00:45:26,025
averaged over September twenty
twenty six, on a one degree grid.

671
00:45:26,375 --> 00:45:29,520
A plain average of all
the grid boxes gives

672
00:45:29,520 --> 00:45:32,300
about seven point one degrees Celsius.

673
00:45:33,800 --> 00:45:38,325
Weighted by cosine phi, it's about
sixteen point three degrees.

674
00:45:39,825 --> 00:45:43,558
The plain average is more
than nine degrees too cold,

675
00:45:43,558 --> 00:45:46,587
because the many thin
boxes near the poles,

676
00:45:46,587 --> 00:45:49,828
over the cold Arctic
and the frozen Antarctic,

677
00:45:49,828 --> 00:45:52,575
count as much as the big tropical ones.

678
00:45:52,925 --> 00:45:57,800
This cosine weighting is how global
average temperatures are worked out.

679
00:45:58,600 --> 00:46:04,185
To sum up, a point on or around the Earth
is pinned down by spherical coordinates:

680
00:46:04,185 --> 00:46:07,250
its distance from the
centre, and two angles.

681
00:46:07,600 --> 00:46:11,184
On the ground, that distance
is the Earth's radius,

682
00:46:11,184 --> 00:46:15,400
about six thousand four hundred
kilometres, which we call a.

683
00:46:15,750 --> 00:46:21,500
Latitude, phi, is the angle up from the
equator plane, measured at the centre.

684
00:46:21,850 --> 00:46:24,947
The colatitude is ninety
degrees minus phi,

685
00:46:24,947 --> 00:46:29,125
measured down from the pole, and it
swaps sine and cosine.

686
00:46:29,475 --> 00:46:34,475
A parallel is a circle of constant
latitude, round the Earth's axis.

687
00:46:34,825 --> 00:46:39,825
A meridian is a half circle from pole
to pole, round the Earth's centre.

688
00:46:40,175 --> 00:46:43,457
Longitude, lambda, is the angle
round the axis,

689
00:46:43,457 --> 00:46:47,925
from the prime meridian through
Greenwich, positive to the east.

690
00:46:48,275 --> 00:46:51,051
Two slices give the position arrow:

691
00:46:51,051 --> 00:46:55,254
x is the radius times cosine
phi times cosine lambda,

692
00:46:55,254 --> 00:46:59,299
y is the radius times cosine
phi times sine lambda,

693
00:46:59,299 --> 00:47:02,075
and z is the radius times sine phi.

694
00:47:02,425 --> 00:47:08,005
The ground is carried east at capital
Omega, times the radius, times cosine phi:

695
00:47:08,005 --> 00:47:13,375
about four hundred and sixty four point
six metres per second at the equator.

696
00:47:13,725 --> 00:47:16,795
A step north
is the radius times delta phi,

697
00:47:16,795 --> 00:47:21,150
about a hundred and eleven
kilometres per degree, everywhere.

698
00:47:21,500 --> 00:47:24,789
A step east
is the radius, times cosine phi,

699
00:47:24,789 --> 00:47:28,750
times delta lambda, and it
shrinks towards the poles.

700
00:47:29,100 --> 00:47:33,165
So a small patch has an area
of the radius squared,

701
00:47:33,165 --> 00:47:35,875
times cosine phi, d phi, d lambda.

702
00:47:36,225 --> 00:47:40,095
Adding up strips shows that
the band from the equator to

703
00:47:40,095 --> 00:47:43,827
latitude phi holds a share
sine phi of its hemisphere,

704
00:47:43,827 --> 00:47:48,250
which is why global averages
weight each latitude by cosine phi.

705
00:47:48,600 --> 00:47:51,762
In Part 4, we'll stand
at a weather station,

706
00:47:51,762 --> 00:47:56,650
and see how its own east, north
and up arrows turn as the air moves.

707
00:47:57,450 --> 00:48:01,608
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

708
00:48:01,608 --> 00:48:03,650
so watch out for the others.

709
00:48:04,000 --> 00:48:08,397
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

710
00:48:08,397 --> 00:48:12,050
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

711
00:48:12,400 --> 00:48:17,228
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

712
00:48:17,228 --> 00:48:20,050
are on the website that
goes with this video.
